WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:20.860
<v Guillaume Hachez>Salut. Moi, c'est Guillaume Hachez. Bienvenue dans Septante Minutes Avec. Pour ce premier numéro, j'ai eu la chance d'interviewer Alexander De Croo, le Vice-Premier Ministre belge et Ministre de l'Agenda numérique.

1
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:30.040
<v Guillaume Hachez>On a parlé pendant une heure de sujets comme l'intelligence artificielle, le manque de femmes dans la tech en Belgique, l'inertie de l'enseignement obligatoire.

2
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.760
<v Guillaume Hachez>Pour que cette interview soit accessible au plus grand nombre, j'ai décidé de la faire en anglais et j'ai aussi mis un transcript dans les notes de l'émission.

3
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.620
<v Guillaume Hachez>Bonne écoute. Welcome everyone, welcome to the show. I'm here with my very first guest ever, Alexander De Croo. Welcome to the show.

4
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.600
<v Alexander De Croo>Thank you. It's a pleasure to be here.

5
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.360
<v Guillaume Hachez>Can you please perhaps introduce yourself for those who don't know you?

6
00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:05.980
<v Alexander De Croo>Alexander De Croo. I'm Belgium's Deputy Prime Minister. I'm also Minister for what is called the Digital Agenda, so for the digital revolution.

7
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.440
<v Alexander De Croo>I'm also Minister for Development Cooperation, which is a completely different topic,

8
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:19.500
<v Alexander De Croo>but there is a strong junction between those two because if you look at development efforts throughout the world, digital actually plays a very important role in that.

9
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:25.440
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm. That was actually the context for which we went to the United Nations back in September.

10
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:32.440
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, what something which is called Digital for Development is something in which Belgium has invested a lot over the last years.

11
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:44.420
<v Alexander De Croo>And I think that the digital investments, especially in Africa, are today the most important investments that have ever taken place there and that hold the most promise

12
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.460
<v Alexander De Croo>for finally lifting a maximum number of people out of poverty.

13
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:56.520
<v Guillaume Hachez>Awesome. Well, we're gonna talk about all that. But before we really get into it, let's start off with kind of a silly question.

14
00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:07.240
<v Guillaume Hachez>You are Belgium's first ever Minister of Digital Agenda. You very clearly care very passionately for topics like innovation, entrepreneurship, AI,

15
00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:14.360
<v Guillaume Hachez>women in tech, and it might also be worth mentioning that you are also rocking a Google Pixel 2 as your main smartphone.

16
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.540
<v Guillaume Hachez>So, Mr. De Croo, let's get this out of the way. Are you one of us? Are you a nerd?

17
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.820
<v Alexander De Croo>Let's call it geek-

18
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:24.320
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay. Okay

19
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.100
<v Alexander De Croo>... which is maybe a bit more middle of the road.

20
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:27.440
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right.

21
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.300
<v Alexander De Croo>But yes, as a-

22
00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:28.420
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay

23
00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:41.160
<v Alexander De Croo>... as a kid, I remember when I was, I think I was 12 years old, so that was beginning of the '80s, my parents, who have a law office, installed a small computer network.

24
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:52.700
<v Alexander De Croo>And what I was doing is that I was always watching over the shoulder of the technicians installing it, taking notes of what they were doing, and then doing it myself afterwards.

25
00:02:53.400 --> 00:03:01.780
<v Alexander De Croo>And so I've always been involved with technology and fiddling and playing with PCs and so on.

26
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:08.850
<v Alexander De Croo>And though I have been for a long, long time a fan of BlackBerry, and for a long time-

27
00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:08.940
<v Guillaume Hachez>Oh

28
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:19.090
<v Alexander De Croo>... continued using a BlackBerry even when the smartphones were coming, well, at some point I understood that I needed to move and, yes, I'm a fan of-

29
00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.450
<v Guillaume Hachez>When did you do the switch?

30
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.840
<v Alexander De Croo>I think I did the switch, must have been 2011 or something. I think I-

31
00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:27.960
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay

32
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:28.690
<v Alexander De Croo>... I stuck-

33
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.829
<v Guillaume Hachez>I think it was still okay back then

34
00:03:29.829 --> 00:03:33.800
<v Alexander De Croo>... I stuck with buttons for a long, long, long time- ... and then I made the switch to-

35
00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:33.910
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right

36
00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:43.840
<v Alexander De Croo>... to typing on glass. But I like the Android system and, when a new Google phone comes out, in general, I try to get it.

37
00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:43.910
<v Guillaume Hachez>Awesome.

38
00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:48.470
<v Alexander De Croo>And now this one, to get the Pixel 2, I had to parallel import it from-

39
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:48.780
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah

40
00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:49.680
<v Alexander De Croo>... through the UK, I think.

41
00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.130
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right then. Is it fair to say that you are Belgium's first ever geek minister?

42
00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.040
<v Alexander De Croo>I don't know. I guess it's not the main characteristic, but-

43
00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:01.430
<v Guillaume Hachez>No, it's not

44
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.320
<v Alexander De Croo>... I have a strong interest in technology, and I use it a lot.

45
00:04:05.329 --> 00:04:05.730
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right. Cool.

46
00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:13.560
<v Alexander De Croo>And I believe that there is a story that needs to be told more often, is that in general, technology improves our lives.

47
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.930
<v Alexander De Croo>Though there are side effects which we need to handle,

48
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.680
<v Alexander De Croo>in general, technology is a positive thing. And,

49
00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:33.219
<v Alexander De Croo>the whole mindset in Europe that you have today, where there is actually some anxiety towards technology and there is a lot of negative thinking around technology is, I think, is a big issue.

50
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:42.660
<v Alexander De Croo>We don't have to be naive, but we have to be quite clear on the positive impact that technology can have in our lives.

51
00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:50.980
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm. It's kinda striking, the gap between politicians and technology. You don't always see an overlap.

52
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:58.600
<v Guillaume Hachez>You don't always see politicians actually talking about technology, talking about topics like artificial intelligence, and

53
00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:08.200
<v Guillaume Hachez>we've been seeing some of that those past few years, for instance, with you talking a lot about artificial intelligence and those topics.

54
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.000
<v Guillaume Hachez>Emmanuel Macron as well has been really focused on the topic of AI.

55
00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:24.040
<v Guillaume Hachez>We're gonna get back to that in a moment, but before we get into that, before you got into politics, you were in a startup yourself. You were-

56
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:24.210
<v Alexander De Croo>Yes

57
00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:35.220
<v Guillaume Hachez>... an entrepreneur yourself. You've been arguing that governments should adopt a more entrepreneurial mindset, and that this is something that was lacking today.

58
00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:43.980
<v Guillaume Hachez>How do you personally feel that your entrepreneurial mindset has affected your career as a politician and maybe, like, this-

59
00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:44.180
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm

60
00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:45.510
<v Guillaume Hachez>... current administration as a whole?

61
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:56.000
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, I think there is more analogies than people think between being an entrepreneur and being a politician, because in the end, as an entrepreneur, at the start,

62
00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:07.912
<v Alexander De Croo>the first people you will convince, the first products that you will sell- Very often it is much more based on you as a person than on exactly the product or the service you have developed.

63
00:06:08.132 --> 00:06:11.052
<v Alexander De Croo>It's very often putting yourself as a person on the line,

64
00:06:11.712 --> 00:06:21.792
<v Alexander De Croo>which is something you do in politics all the time. In politics, yes, we do have our programs, and we do have our ideas and so on, but in the end, it's still a very personal, people-based thing.

65
00:06:21.932 --> 00:06:28.812
<v Alexander De Croo>And that's something quite recognizable with the entrepreneurial world. Now there are some big differences as well.

66
00:06:29.352 --> 00:06:42.592
<v Alexander De Croo>And the big difference in politics, of course, is that especially in Belgium, you have to find consensus around certain ideas and the pace is much, much slower.

67
00:06:42.772 --> 00:06:52.362
<v Alexander De Croo>Now, I think that governments or public service should ... Well, we today say that we want people to be more entrepreneurial.

68
00:06:52.972 --> 00:07:04.012
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, maybe we have to be more entrepreneurial ourselves as well. And instead of sometimes having lengthy discussions about what might happen, we should maybe just,

69
00:07:04.832 --> 00:07:16.332
<v Alexander De Croo>work more in this sandbox type of approach where we test out certain things, where we know that it's okay to fail, but at least we have tried something out instead of getting stuck in very long discussions.

70
00:07:16.552 --> 00:07:27.352
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm. But so, given your entrepreneurial background, given your kind of mindset that you have, I mean, concretely, like, is there something ...

71
00:07:27.492 --> 00:07:32.022
<v Guillaume Hachez>an example you can give us of something that you think you would not have done, had you n-

72
00:07:32.112 --> 00:07:32.232
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm

73
00:07:32.312 --> 00:07:34.242
<v Guillaume Hachez>... had you not had this entrepreneurial experience?

74
00:07:34.312 --> 00:07:39.102
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, there's a few things. First of all, I've spent 10 years outside of politics-

75
00:07:39.172 --> 00:07:39.562
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm

76
00:07:39.562 --> 00:07:48.872
<v Alexander De Croo>... and that gives me a lot of independence. I mean, I love what I do as a politician and, I would love to continue doing it,

77
00:07:49.472 --> 00:07:55.652
<v Alexander De Croo>but the day that I will not find the energy or that I will not find the impact that I want to have,

78
00:07:56.532 --> 00:07:58.792
<v Alexander De Croo>I know that there's a lot of other things I can do.

79
00:07:58.892 --> 00:08:09.511
<v Alexander De Croo>And that gives me a lot of independence in a political world where I think it's much easier to defend my ideas knowing that I am actually quite independent.

80
00:08:09.612 --> 00:08:21.092
<v Alexander De Croo>Second element is obviously that I have a feel for opportunity. And this is, I think, what distinguishes an entrepreneur

81
00:08:21.552 --> 00:08:31.452
<v Alexander De Croo>from someone who's not thinking as an entrepreneur is that what entrepreneurs do is they see the same as what other people see, but their first reaction is, "We can do this better,

82
00:08:32.172 --> 00:08:33.682
<v Alexander De Croo>and have a better solution for this-"

83
00:08:33.752 --> 00:08:33.782
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm

84
00:08:33.782 --> 00:08:44.172
<v Alexander De Croo>"... and have something that is more convenient or more adapted to the needs of people." And that I think is something that as a politician you need to have, and it's something that we lack often in Belgium,

85
00:08:44.652 --> 00:08:50.332
<v Alexander De Croo>is this sense of ambition and the sense of doing things better and not being happy with the status quo.

86
00:08:50.412 --> 00:09:03.622
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right. A couple of months ago it made the news that, of all the money that was raised by Belgian startups, I think the total was around 327 million euros for 2017.

87
00:09:03.652 --> 00:09:03.772
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

88
00:09:04.382 --> 00:09:08.722
<v Guillaume Hachez>It made the news that only 8% of that money was raised by Walloon startups.

89
00:09:09.012 --> 00:09:09.142
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

90
00:09:09.272 --> 00:09:19.172
<v Guillaume Hachez>So it kind of becomes apparent that there's sort of an entrepreneurial divide here in Belgium where, you know, only 8% of the money was raised in Wallonia.

91
00:09:19.622 --> 00:09:19.652
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm.

92
00:09:19.652 --> 00:09:21.512
<v Guillaume Hachez>What does that say really about Belgium and-

93
00:09:22.312 --> 00:09:23.072
<v Alexander De Croo>I

94
00:09:24.532 --> 00:09:37.832
<v Alexander De Croo>think we should honestly stop to divide our country in three. And honestly, I don't care that much because I can imagine that you will have people who grew up in Wallonia,

95
00:09:38.552 --> 00:09:43.592
<v Alexander De Croo>went to university in Brussels, and then maybe started a company in Antwerp.

96
00:09:44.312 --> 00:09:51.062
<v Alexander De Croo>Now, what is that? Then in the statistics we will say, "Oh, this is money being raised in Antwerp," but then, f-

97
00:09:51.792 --> 00:09:59.632
<v Alexander De Croo>from my perspective, we're all Belgians and, I'm not a big fan of the tribalism that some people are trying to install in Belgium.

98
00:09:59.682 --> 00:09:59.692
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm.

99
00:09:59.692 --> 00:10:10.492
<v Alexander De Croo>The good news, I think, is that a lot of money is being raised. The good news is that you see kind of hubs being created throughout Belgium.

100
00:10:11.232 --> 00:10:25.552
<v Alexander De Croo>And that finally being an entrepreneur is seen as something aspirational, and that a lot of young people actually aspire to be an entrepreneur, and that has been different in Belgium in the past, so we can only be happy with that.

101
00:10:25.762 --> 00:10:35.632
<v Guillaume Hachez>More on that, I think back to what you were just saying just now, Belgians have this kind of reputation that they don't really like taking risks.

102
00:10:35.672 --> 00:10:41.072
<v Guillaume Hachez>Maybe that is something that's starting to change. But, like, according to Eurostat, like, the,

103
00:10:41.862 --> 00:10:50.792
<v Guillaume Hachez>the business birth rate, the company creation rate for Belgium is around, like, 3.6%, which is, like, the lowest in Europe.

104
00:10:51.092 --> 00:10:58.832
<v Guillaume Hachez>In a way one might say that, like you were saying, like, entrepreneurs are kind of a rare species around here.

105
00:10:58.872 --> 00:10:59.112
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

106
00:10:59.212 --> 00:11:09.552
<v Guillaume Hachez>Do you think that is something that will change? Do you think that is something that is already changing maybe? And how do we provoke this change?

107
00:11:09.592 --> 00:11:16.032
<v Guillaume Hachez>How do we share this mindset and encourage young people to start a business? Like, what is the key to that?

108
00:11:16.872 --> 00:11:30.012
<v Alexander De Croo>I think that first of all, you need some good role models, and we start to have those role models. And I often say it when I speak in front of an entrepreneurial crowd, is if you want ...

109
00:11:30.032 --> 00:11:36.292
<v Alexander De Croo>You cannot let the discussion about our economy or about entrepreneurship, you cannot just leave it to politicians.

110
00:11:36.832 --> 00:11:43.052
<v Alexander De Croo>You need to be part of that debate as well, and if you want entrepreneurship to be respected, don't hesitate to go on stage yourself.

111
00:11:43.572 --> 00:11:52.332
<v Alexander De Croo>And I have two young kids, they're six and nine, and obviously they're very inspired by our national football team, which is great.

112
00:11:52.932 --> 00:11:58.082
<v Alexander De Croo>And why are they inspired by the national football team? Because they see how good they are. I would

113
00:11:58.952 --> 00:12:05.672
<v Alexander De Croo>love that when they're 16 years old, they will see how good our entrepreneurs are, and that they get inspired by our entrepreneurs as well.

114
00:12:06.112 --> 00:12:15.602
<v Alexander De Croo>I think this is one element. Second element is that obviously- As an employee in Belgium, you have a quite good situation.

115
00:12:16.222 --> 00:12:28.462
<v Alexander De Croo>Some people will call it the golden cage. And it's true. It's not so easy to say goodbye to the employee status and to say, "I will start from zero and take a lot of risk."

116
00:12:28.522 --> 00:12:35.342
<v Alexander De Croo>I remember that when I did that, a lot of my friends said, "Well, you're stupid." I mean, "Why have you done it this way?

117
00:12:35.962 --> 00:12:38.152
<v Alexander De Croo>You could have a much better life in some way."

118
00:12:38.182 --> 00:12:40.081
<v Guillaume Hachez>I've had this experience as well, so I can relate.

119
00:12:40.402 --> 00:12:47.762
<v Alexander De Croo>So it's ... And that's what I often say to people who say, "Well, one day we'll be an entrepreneur." I tell them, "Well, don't wait too long.

120
00:12:48.422 --> 00:12:59.442
<v Alexander De Croo>If you want to do it, then you should do it right now because the longer you wait, the harder it is going to be to leave the financial stability and the financial security which you have as an employee."

121
00:12:59.862 --> 00:13:10.022
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm. Mm-hmm. This is kind of a very personal question and a very personal example, but I have actually started to visit schools and to actually talk about entrepreneurship-

122
00:13:10.342 --> 00:13:10.402
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah

123
00:13:10.682 --> 00:13:15.322
<v Guillaume Hachez>... and what it's like and what it means, what it does to you as a person.

124
00:13:16.002 --> 00:13:24.552
<v Guillaume Hachez>So far it's been kind of a great experience, but it's been kind of tainted by the fact that whenever I get in touch with a school that's in the general section-

125
00:13:24.962 --> 00:13:25.082
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm

126
00:13:25.722 --> 00:13:36.962
<v Guillaume Hachez>... you can feel that they're not very receptive at all. Like, they don't understand what we're talking about. And like, the very first question they ask is, "Oh, but we don't really have a computer class here."

127
00:13:36.982 --> 00:13:46.982
<v Guillaume Hachez>And so you have to explain that it's about entrepreneurship, that it's about tracing your own path. And then they tell you, "Oh, that may be for the economics class, economical sciences."

128
00:13:47.582 --> 00:13:56.052
<v Guillaume Hachez>And they're really not that receptive to the message, and it's kind of hard to actually reach those kids and-

129
00:13:56.782 --> 00:13:56.792
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm

130
00:13:56.792 --> 00:14:04.922
<v Guillaume Hachez>... deliver the message to them. So, again, this is a very personal question, but what is the ... What is the way you would suggest taking to-

131
00:14:05.622 --> 00:14:06.052
<v Alexander De Croo>Look, I-

132
00:14:06.052 --> 00:14:06.672
<v Guillaume Hachez>... reach those kids?

133
00:14:06.922 --> 00:14:17.782
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah. I'm not so sure that classes about entrepreneurship have the biggest impact. I think what has the most impact is to just show people what entrepreneurs are doing.

134
00:14:18.542 --> 00:14:26.922
<v Alexander De Croo>And so very often we will talk about the fact that we need to have internships for kids and so on, and I think that that's good, that's a good thing.

135
00:14:26.962 --> 00:14:38.651
<v Alexander De Croo>That can definitely help. But what would also help is that when our teachers would also do entrepreneur internships, because our teachers are very often quite far away-

136
00:14:38.662 --> 00:14:38.772
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right

137
00:14:38.802 --> 00:14:55.332
<v Alexander De Croo>... from the economic reality. And that explains, I think, part of the answers that you have gotten. So, today there is too much of a gap between the educational world and the entrepreneurial world.

138
00:14:55.842 --> 00:15:05.582
<v Alexander De Croo>And you see it in the typical high schools, but you also see it in other domains. I mean, if today we will talk about artificial intelligence and so on-

139
00:15:05.682 --> 00:15:05.822
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm

140
00:15:05.832 --> 00:15:13.942
<v Alexander De Croo>... there is in Belgium today not one decent university degree on artificial intelligence. I mean, that is appalling.

141
00:15:14.542 --> 00:15:24.752
<v Alexander De Croo>I don't understand why there is not more reactivity from our universities, even in big data, and big data is ... I mean, we've been talking about it for some years now.

142
00:15:24.762 --> 00:15:29.221
<v Alexander De Croo>It's quite hard to find a university specialization in big data.

143
00:15:29.232 --> 00:15:29.262
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

144
00:15:29.262 --> 00:15:30.482
<v Alexander De Croo>No one is providing it to them.

145
00:15:30.692 --> 00:15:32.822
<v Guillaume Hachez>So, yeah, so there's a gap, a growing gap between-

146
00:15:32.862 --> 00:15:40.072
<v Alexander De Croo>There's a gigantic gap, and our universities are really lacking this kind of responsive-

147
00:15:40.371 --> 00:15:40.381
<v Guillaume Hachez>Response, yeah

148
00:15:40.381 --> 00:15:42.712
<v Alexander De Croo>... answer in that domain, yeah.

149
00:15:43.242 --> 00:15:55.562
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right. Well, more on that. Let's talk about AI for a bit. I think it's, you know, it's pretty fair to say that our current economy, our current system is built around the idea of human labor.

150
00:15:55.602 --> 00:16:07.962
<v Guillaume Hachez>People work and they get paid for it. With the rise of AI technologies, it's becoming more and more apparent that this is something that's probably about to change, like the status quo is about to change a lot.

151
00:16:08.002 --> 00:16:18.582
<v Guillaume Hachez>We're talking about a revolution that would affect, like, healthcare, economy, democracy, and we're still hearing fairly little about it from our politicians.

152
00:16:18.702 --> 00:16:23.422
<v Guillaume Hachez>Like, I don't hear Charles Michel talking about it. I don't hear-

153
00:16:23.942 --> 00:16:24.062
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

154
00:16:24.082 --> 00:16:27.401
<v Guillaume Hachez>... the minister of economy in Brussels or in Wallonia talking about it.

155
00:16:27.841 --> 00:16:27.992
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

156
00:16:28.221 --> 00:16:35.112
<v Guillaume Hachez>Or maybe they did, but I didn't hear it. How do you explain this, this absence of-

157
00:16:35.182 --> 00:16:35.572
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, I think-

158
00:16:35.882 --> 00:16:36.162
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah.

159
00:16:36.182 --> 00:16:44.782
<v Alexander De Croo>... I think that there is some talking about it, but again, too often on the negative side. So this is going to destroy jobs.

160
00:16:45.042 --> 00:16:55.732
<v Alexander De Croo>We have to be careful about the ethical dimension. And we should see it as a threat, where I think we should have the opposite approach. First of all,

161
00:16:56.462 --> 00:16:59.622
<v Alexander De Croo>does AI, does that mean that work is going to disappear? No,

162
00:17:00.291 --> 00:17:11.842
<v Alexander De Croo>not at all. I don't believe that story at all, and I hear those stories where we say, "Well, there's no meaningful work that will remain, and so the only solution is to give everyone a universal basic income."

163
00:17:11.942 --> 00:17:21.302
<v Alexander De Croo>I do not believe in universal basic income at all, and I'm happy that the experiment in Finland has basically proven that it is not a viable option.

164
00:17:21.962 --> 00:17:31.782
<v Alexander De Croo>The problem is that I'm not against the fact that you give people a certain allowance, but giving it without any productive dimension to it I think is wrong.

165
00:17:31.902 --> 00:17:48.662
<v Alexander De Croo>So that is why we developed together with the World Economic Forum something else, which is the universal right to learn, which basically is a reskilling program where you can give people a certain income, but in exchange for reskilling.

166
00:17:48.862 --> 00:17:55.942
<v Alexander De Croo>I mean, at... You need at least to have some productivity improvement element to it, otherwise economically it doesn't make sense at all.

167
00:17:56.542 --> 00:18:10.562
<v Alexander De Croo>Now, on AI, AI is just going to be like a lot of other technologies. It is a tool that we use, and it's probably going to be a quite powerful tool, but still it remains a tool.

168
00:18:11.302 --> 00:18:26.622
<v Alexander De Croo>And the tool is complementary to our skills. And so I think that the real question we should ask is when you are in times of machine learning, the question is then maybe we should learn what machines will not learn on the short term.

169
00:18:26.702 --> 00:18:39.162
<v Alexander De Croo>And do I think that AI today, especially in well-defined environments, yes, has a lot of potential, a potential, and it's partly already there.

170
00:18:39.882 --> 00:18:54.282
<v Alexander De Croo>I mean, we're still far away from replacing our capability of complex problem-solving, our capability on innovation and creativity, and our capabilities on emotions and on empathy and so on.

171
00:18:54.782 --> 00:19:03.282
<v Alexander De Croo>So I believe that in an AI world, probably we will, in our education system, emphasize much more the very human elements.

172
00:19:04.082 --> 00:19:11.362
<v Alexander De Croo>Very often when we speak about how to change education in the fourth industrial revolution, people talk about smart boards and tablets and so on.

173
00:19:12.482 --> 00:19:17.502
<v Alexander De Croo>I'm not so convinced about that. I don't think that's the solution. I think the solution, if you want

174
00:19:18.282 --> 00:19:28.782
<v Alexander De Croo>everyone to be able to have value added as a person in an AI world, is to just focus much more on the very human elements, and that is a bit of contradiction.

175
00:19:28.952 --> 00:19:40.952
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm. The education system has been kind of very slow to evolve and like sometimes I look at schools and I kind of wonder if they have really changed compared to what they were like 30 years ago, and-

176
00:19:41.002 --> 00:19:43.232
<v Alexander De Croo>No, they haven't. And-

177
00:19:44.102 --> 00:19:44.412
<v Guillaume Hachez>That's the answer.

178
00:19:44.412 --> 00:19:48.042
<v Alexander De Croo>... that's what I see every day with my kids. My kids go to the same school where I went

179
00:19:48.802 --> 00:19:58.402
<v Alexander De Croo>X years ago. And that can give you a warm feeling to go back to your own school, but it gives me less of a warm feeling to see that not that much has changed, unfortunately.

180
00:19:59.082 --> 00:20:09.622
<v Alexander De Croo>And what I have noticed is that change in education is very slow, and there's a number of good reasons why it is slow.

181
00:20:09.982 --> 00:20:23.861
<v Alexander De Croo>Now, we can say, "Oh, it's slow and it's a problem," or we could use another approach, which is what we are doing in Belgium, is to say, "Well, let's then do things outside of the educational system, to at least prove that things are possible."

182
00:20:23.922 --> 00:20:39.582
<v Alexander De Croo>And this is what we did with the digital skills fund, where we fund 40 projects every year. It's 6 million euros every year that we invest in projects that are combining digital and education, but that are outside of the schools.

183
00:20:39.842 --> 00:20:52.162
<v Alexander De Croo>A lot of them are quite successful. My feeling is that we will prove outside of the schools that they work, and once we have proven that, I hope that the schools will take them back inside at some point.

184
00:20:52.202 --> 00:20:52.382
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right.

185
00:20:52.782 --> 00:20:53.562
<v Alexander De Croo>Because I think that

186
00:20:54.362 --> 00:21:00.442
<v Alexander De Croo>all those initiatives are great. They have one big drawback, is that there's a social filter. You--

187
00:21:01.122 --> 00:21:08.782
<v Alexander De Croo>We will send our kids there because we know it's important, but people who have no interest in technology will not send their kids there, and that's a problem.

188
00:21:08.942 --> 00:21:20.962
<v Alexander De Croo>The general school system is one gigantic social equalizer, and that's a good thing. So the current system of doing it outside of the schools, it's better than nothing, but it has some issues.

189
00:21:22.302 --> 00:21:34.182
<v Guillaume Hachez>Let's get back to AI for a bit. It made the news that China and the United States have been making a lot of progress in the field of AI, and fairly little is happening in Europe.

190
00:21:34.342 --> 00:21:34.612
<v Alexander De Croo>Yes. That's true.

191
00:21:34.632 --> 00:21:47.362
<v Guillaume Hachez>And because of the very important place of AI in our future, or at least the perspectives we have right now, some fear that this means we will become very dependent on China and on the United States,

192
00:21:48.222 --> 00:21:50.951
<v Guillaume Hachez>as an economy, as a culture, as a so-

193
00:21:50.951 --> 00:21:50.992
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

194
00:21:50.992 --> 00:21:55.842
<v Guillaume Hachez>... as a society. Do you agree with this sentiment? And how do we prevent this from happening?

195
00:21:56.162 --> 00:22:08.282
<v Alexander De Croo>I think that if you look at the state of play today, yes. There's, especially United States, China, and Israel are the places where things are happening in AI, and it's different models.

196
00:22:08.342 --> 00:22:18.842
<v Alexander De Croo>I mean, the US and the Chinese model is very different. One is state-driven, the other is less so. And the Israeli system is driven by their military investment.

197
00:22:19.382 --> 00:22:29.342
<v Alexander De Croo>Still, we see that some public investment plays a role everywhere. And so I think that in Europe now is the moment, and I think that Macron talked about it-

198
00:22:29.752 --> 00:22:29.781
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm.

199
00:22:29.802 --> 00:22:52.062
<v Alexander De Croo>... to create a common investment pool for AI. And though we have some backlog on it, Europe has an interesting environment to experiment with AI, and that is because AI is working on data.

200
00:22:52.202 --> 00:23:08.802
<v Alexander De Croo>And there is a lot of discussion about GDPR, but I consider that GDPR is a good thing because GDPR is coming to a balanced agreement between the public and economic actors on how to use data.

201
00:23:09.502 --> 00:23:19.882
<v Alexander De Croo>And so finding a balanced way of how to handle data, I think is the basic condition for making AI work in an acceptable way.

202
00:23:20.822 --> 00:23:28.802
<v Alexander De Croo>And so I think that Europe has better done its groundwork in what the environment is, but has some backlog in the technology itself.

203
00:23:28.902 --> 00:23:40.062
<v Alexander De Croo>But at least the groundwork, I think, is a better one. I fear that a lot of things that have been happening throughout the world in AI are interesting, but you could lose the buy-in of the public

204
00:23:40.942 --> 00:23:44.422
<v Alexander De Croo>quite rapidly, and that would be a problem.

205
00:23:44.862 --> 00:23:53.202
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right. So a lot of groundwork has been done in terms of legislation, in terms of preparing for this revolution, at least on the European side.

206
00:23:53.242 --> 00:23:56.622
<v Guillaume Hachez>But back to what you were saying earlier in terms of education, in terms of-

207
00:23:56.812 --> 00:23:57.022
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

208
00:23:57.032 --> 00:24:05.602
<v Guillaume Hachez>... academia. According to the OECD, in Belgium, there's only 1.1% of our students who are studying ICT.

209
00:24:05.922 --> 00:24:06.022
<v Alexander De Croo>Yes.

210
00:24:06.082 --> 00:24:15.402
<v Guillaume Hachez>That is the lowest ratio in the entire list of OECD countries. It seems like studying computing isn't exactly all the rage here in Belgium.

211
00:24:15.942 --> 00:24:20.026
<v Guillaume Hachez>As the Minister of Digital Agenda, how do you explain that?

212
00:24:20.066 --> 00:24:20.095
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

213
00:24:20.095 --> 00:24:27.156
<v Guillaume Hachez>And also, in a world driven by AI, isn't this a massive problem, the fact that we're not producing a lot of computing experts?

214
00:24:27.246 --> 00:24:33.346
<v Alexander De Croo>I think it's... Yes, I mean, it's a problem that we are that low. Of course, you have to look at it a bit broader.

215
00:24:33.706 --> 00:24:37.406
<v Alexander De Croo>If you look at people who are active in ICT today,

216
00:24:38.126 --> 00:24:51.946
<v Alexander De Croo>while a lot of them actually did not study computer sciences, but a lot of them studied civil engineering, studied other types of hard science, and then made the transition into the information technology world.

217
00:24:51.986 --> 00:25:02.426
<v Alexander De Croo>So yes, that statistic is a worrying one, but if you look at it in a broader sense and you look at all STEM educations, there the situation is not that dramatic.

218
00:25:02.526 --> 00:25:14.586
<v Alexander De Croo>Still, there's a huge effort to be done to convince more people that studying in STEM topics is a good thing, and especially for girls, and we'll talk about it later.

219
00:25:14.616 --> 00:25:14.706
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah, we're gonna get to that.

220
00:25:14.706 --> 00:25:24.406
<v Alexander De Croo>I guess it is today, I think the main hurdle we see in digitization today is access to people.

221
00:25:24.486 --> 00:25:25.086
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm-hmm.

222
00:25:25.226 --> 00:25:36.086
<v Alexander De Croo>And then I guess that a sensible way of organizing migration could be a solution, and it is a discussion which is a very delicate one in Belgium today.

223
00:25:36.386 --> 00:25:36.586
<v Guillaume Hachez>Sure.

224
00:25:36.706 --> 00:25:44.426
<v Alexander De Croo>But the talent migration is something for which we too often close our doors, and we are wrong in doing that, I think.

225
00:25:45.326 --> 00:25:51.586
<v Guillaume Hachez>You mentioned one of the main points I wanna talk about today, which is women in tech, women in computer sciences.

226
00:25:52.706 --> 00:26:05.886
<v Guillaume Hachez>We don't produce a lot of computer scientists, but among those we don't have a lot of women. I think the percentage of women students in ICT for Belgium is around 7.2%, which,

227
00:26:06.746 --> 00:26:11.786
<v Guillaume Hachez>good news, or bad news, is also the lowest percentage in the OECD.

228
00:26:12.446 --> 00:26:24.026
<v Guillaume Hachez>So in that case too, Belgium is ranking last. How do you explain that? How do you explain the very low participation of women in ICT, in digital, in computer science?

229
00:26:24.466 --> 00:26:38.516
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, I think that what you explain about Belgium is a general phenomenon. Some countries do it better. I see, for example, some Eastern European countries do it actually a lot, a lot, a lot better than Belgium.

230
00:26:39.006 --> 00:26:48.026
<v Alexander De Croo>But it's a general issue, and the general issue... Actually, if you look in history, at the beginning of computer sciences, the problem was not there.

231
00:26:48.086 --> 00:27:01.236
<v Alexander De Croo>There were actually a lot of women active in computer science in the '60s and in the '70s. That changed at some point due to the personal computer, because the personal computer brought it to the homes and

232
00:27:02.006 --> 00:27:04.146
<v Alexander De Croo>gave this little geeky side to it.

233
00:27:04.406 --> 00:27:04.416
<v Guillaume Hachez>Mm.

234
00:27:04.426 --> 00:27:09.256
<v Alexander De Croo>Because you would have, just like I was, 12-year-olds fiddling with PCs.

235
00:27:09.326 --> 00:27:10.306
<v Guillaume Hachez>Oh, so it is your fault.

236
00:27:10.426 --> 00:27:19.966
<v Alexander De Croo>It is, maybe. Well, I was part of it. You would have 12-year-olds fiddling with PCs, and at that point for girls, that is not an attractive thing to do.

237
00:27:20.046 --> 00:27:21.446
<v Alexander De Croo>They are busy with other things.

238
00:27:21.506 --> 00:27:21.926
<v Guillaume Hachez>Is it not?

239
00:27:22.246 --> 00:27:25.046
<v Alexander De Croo>Also, also because of bias, because of-

240
00:27:25.166 --> 00:27:25.176
<v Guillaume Hachez>Sure.

241
00:27:25.176 --> 00:27:28.116
<v Alexander De Croo>... gender bias, which we install in a number of-

242
00:27:28.166 --> 00:27:28.666
<v Guillaume Hachez>The, the-

243
00:27:28.696 --> 00:27:29.186
<v Alexander De Croo>... number of ways.

244
00:27:29.326 --> 00:27:33.006
<v Guillaume Hachez>... so-called Hollywood depiction of hackers and of the geeks.

245
00:27:33.046 --> 00:27:46.066
<v Alexander De Croo>Exactly, but so that image is not an attractive one to girls, and girls get conditioned through bias in a very different way, which means that when you would have girls going to university at the age of 18 years old,

246
00:27:46.906 --> 00:27:55.416
<v Alexander De Croo>they have a backlog compared to boys who have been playing with these things, and so there's a strong rejection.

247
00:27:55.866 --> 00:28:05.686
<v Alexander De Croo>Now, there have been techniques used in that to attract other profiles into information technological studies.

248
00:28:06.346 --> 00:28:17.226
<v Alexander De Croo>For example, at MIT today, which is quite a hard one to get into, in the beginning, the first steps of programming is using Scratch, which is something made for kids.

249
00:28:17.796 --> 00:28:17.906
<v Guillaume Hachez>Sure.

250
00:28:17.906 --> 00:28:27.546
<v Alexander De Croo>But using Scratch makes it a bit less geeky at the start and easier to grasp the general concepts that are being used.

251
00:28:27.946 --> 00:28:39.246
<v Alexander De Croo>So I would think that in Belgium, this is what we need to do. We need to make it more practical, more fun, less of a geeky thing.

252
00:28:39.926 --> 00:28:48.946
<v Alexander De Croo>And what I see is when I visit the CoderDojos and the others, is that we see gigantic progress. I mean, there's a lot of girls there, and that's a good thing.

253
00:28:49.026 --> 00:28:55.346
<v Alexander De Croo>Now, today they are between six and 12, but that will flow through in the years to come.

254
00:28:55.946 --> 00:29:07.226
<v Guillaume Hachez>Sure. And so what's kind of very apparent, I had kind of the same thought process as you, which is that, you know, when I look at an auditorium of computer scientists at ULB, I can see very clearly the,

255
00:29:07.806 --> 00:29:12.526
<v Guillaume Hachez>the pathway they took from playing video games on their computer when they were-

256
00:29:12.536 --> 00:29:12.546
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah.

257
00:29:12.546 --> 00:29:20.406
<v Guillaume Hachez>... fairly young to learning PHP when they were 14, and then really getting into it, and then identifying as a nerd, as a geek.

258
00:29:20.466 --> 00:29:31.516
<v Guillaume Hachez>And so, yeah, the entryway to this geekiness, to this computing world is a boys' gateway.

259
00:29:31.526 --> 00:29:31.536
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

260
00:29:31.886 --> 00:29:44.825
<v Guillaume Hachez>And some have argued, like you do, that adding programming classes, that adding programming to the education world would add a new gateway that would be universal.

261
00:29:44.996 --> 00:29:51.386
<v Alexander De Croo>I think so. And there, I mean, there are some surprising things. If I remember that when I was in high school,

262
00:29:52.226 --> 00:30:02.166
<v Alexander De Croo>and so that was beginning of the '90s, I learned how to code, and I learned, I think it was Turbo Pascal.

263
00:30:02.506 --> 00:30:02.666
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay.

264
00:30:02.866 --> 00:30:08.666
<v Alexander De Croo>And that gave us a good basis of what coding is, and I remember that-

265
00:30:09.526 --> 00:30:10.226
<v Guillaume Hachez>That was in high school?

266
00:30:10.256 --> 00:30:10.486
<v Alexander De Croo>That was in high school, yes.

267
00:30:10.506 --> 00:30:10.536
<v Guillaume Hachez>Whoa.

268
00:30:10.536 --> 00:30:14.606
<v Alexander De Croo>That was in high school, and I remember that the exam was on paper.

269
00:30:15.016 --> 00:30:15.026
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah.

270
00:30:15.026 --> 00:30:25.918
<v Alexander De Croo>So you basically wrote programming code on paper and then handed out, which is a bit of strange thing. And I loved it. I liked it, and I think I was, well, I was quite good at it.

271
00:30:26.028 --> 00:30:34.718
<v Alexander De Croo>And now if I look at the same school today, and I asked it to the director recently, they don't do it anymore.

272
00:30:35.038 --> 00:30:37.718
<v Alexander De Croo>They don't teach coding. So in the '90s we did it.

273
00:30:37.758 --> 00:30:38.238
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah.

274
00:30:38.248 --> 00:30:39.468
<v Alexander De Croo>And today we don't, which-

275
00:30:39.718 --> 00:30:40.898
<v Guillaume Hachez>How do you explain this situation?

276
00:30:40.908 --> 00:30:52.208
<v Alexander De Croo>It's impossible for me to explain that. Now one of the things that I have often said is that if you look at our school system in Belgium, the way we basically test kids on their,

277
00:30:53.018 --> 00:31:01.998
<v Alexander De Croo>what they call capability of abstract thinking, is that very often today that is still done through Latin and through maths.

278
00:31:02.718 --> 00:31:10.218
<v Alexander De Croo>And, I mean, I did that too. I did four years of Latin. I thought it was horrendous.

279
00:31:10.318 --> 00:31:12.458
<v Guillaume Hachez>I did six years of Latin and I thought it was great.

280
00:31:12.558 --> 00:31:22.858
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah. Okay. Well then you have different views on that. And obviously, yes, it helps you because it gives you a basis of the language which determined our culture, but in the end, why do you,

281
00:31:23.358 --> 00:31:26.438
<v Alexander De Croo>why do so many people force their kids into Latin?

282
00:31:26.478 --> 00:31:39.058
<v Alexander De Croo>Because they know that this is kind of how you get being stimulated in a certain way. But it also rejects a lot of kids, and it rejects a lot of kids, first of all, because they consider it something unuseful.

283
00:31:39.738 --> 00:31:45.758
<v Alexander De Croo>And very often also a lot of parents who have not been exposed to Latin very often will say, "Well, why would we do that?"

284
00:31:45.768 --> 00:31:46.248
<v Guillaume Hachez>Yeah, it's a dead language. Why would we?

285
00:31:46.318 --> 00:31:57.338
<v Alexander De Croo>So it's a dead language. Why would you even bother? I would think that you can achieve exactly the same testing of abstraction capability with programming languages.

286
00:31:57.738 --> 00:32:06.138
<v Alexander De Croo>It's exactly the same. I mean, in the end, it's an abstract structure that you use for certain things, and it's much more useful.

287
00:32:06.858 --> 00:32:14.338
<v Alexander De Croo>Now, I've said that at some occasions, and then I got some angry reactions from Latin teachers. I'm not saying that Latin should disappear.

288
00:32:14.778 --> 00:32:24.158
<v Alexander De Croo>I'm just saying that coding could be another way of testing that and could be a way that appeals to much more kids than the way it's done today.

289
00:32:24.198 --> 00:32:24.878
<v Guillaume Hachez>Including girls.

290
00:32:25.298 --> 00:32:26.248
<v Alexander De Croo>Including girls, yes.

291
00:32:26.248 --> 00:32:27.208
<v Guillaume Hachez>So that's also interesting.

292
00:32:27.738 --> 00:32:39.958
<v Guillaume Hachez>More on that, on the French-speaking side, they are working on this massive reform that I think will only impact high schools at the end of the next decade, I think around 2027 or something.

293
00:32:40.298 --> 00:32:40.498
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah.

294
00:32:40.698 --> 00:32:48.018
<v Guillaume Hachez>And one of the first thing they announced was that Latin would become mandatory for everyone until the age of 15.

295
00:32:48.098 --> 00:33:04.618
<v Guillaume Hachez>And in terms of digital, they want to include a digital aspect to teach a digital culture and all that. But as soon as you start talking about algorithmics, talking about programming, there's still a lot of debate on that front.

296
00:33:04.727 --> 00:33:04.738
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah.

297
00:33:04.738 --> 00:33:06.298
<v Guillaume Hachez>Like a lot of lobbying has been done-

298
00:33:06.398 --> 00:33:06.848
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

299
00:33:07.098 --> 00:33:13.658
<v Guillaume Hachez>... a lot of pro and anti-programming classes. Yeah, again, like I guess that's the same question I was asking you earlier.

300
00:33:13.698 --> 00:33:14.758
<v Guillaume Hachez>Like how do you explain this?

301
00:33:16.078 --> 00:33:16.667
<v Alexander De Croo>I think that-

302
00:33:16.667 --> 00:33:17.258
<v Guillaume Hachez>This need to-

304
00:33:18.098 --> 00:33:21.758
<v Guillaume Hachez>... not be okay with programming and to think that it's such a bad idea when-

305
00:33:21.918 --> 00:33:34.578
<v Alexander De Croo>I don't know. There's a few elements. First of all, in Flanders also we have these educational reforms, and it takes a gigantic amount of time because there's lobbying in all directions, and it's a hard thing to do.

306
00:33:34.607 --> 00:33:43.698
<v Alexander De Croo>And I think it's actually the wrong approach because we are trying to define what we think that kids should learn, and it should be a stable environment for the next 10, 15 years.

307
00:33:44.598 --> 00:33:49.138
<v Alexander De Croo>I'm not sure we can actually define that because we live in such a rapidly changing world.

308
00:33:49.748 --> 00:33:49.878
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

309
00:33:49.878 --> 00:34:01.178
<v Alexander De Croo>I am much more in favor of giving the schools much more autonomy in that and letting the schools fill it in themselves and giving the teachers much more autonomy in that.

310
00:34:01.198 --> 00:34:09.998
<v Alexander De Croo>Today, teachers are basically being transformed into some teaching robots who are good when they fulfill the administration.

311
00:34:10.638 --> 00:34:18.478
<v Alexander De Croo>That's not why you became a teacher. People didn't become a teacher to fill out the administration. They became a teacher because they have a passion to teach something to-

312
00:34:18.518 --> 00:34:18.558
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right

313
00:34:18.578 --> 00:34:27.838
<v Alexander De Croo>... to kids. And giving them more freedom, I think, is actually something that will lead to better quality education than the way it is being organized today.

314
00:34:28.038 --> 00:34:34.578
<v Alexander De Croo>Now on coding and so on, there is a lot of conservative in this.

315
00:34:34.638 --> 00:34:34.648
<v Guillaume Hachez>Right.

316
00:34:34.678 --> 00:34:46.468
<v Alexander De Croo>I think there's also a total lack of knowledge in what the possibilities are. And a lot of thinking like, "Well, you know, but coding is only going to be for a certain part of the population."

317
00:34:46.998 --> 00:34:56.748
<v Alexander De Croo>But computational thinking is the element that we should have, is to at least give people a sense of the structure of these things and how it is being done.

318
00:34:57.458 --> 00:35:06.758
<v Alexander De Croo>I find it, come back to my example, really, really strange that if I compare it to, for example, my kids,

319
00:35:08.018 --> 00:35:15.238
<v Alexander De Croo>I was at school almost at this age closer to computers than they have been up to now, which is really, really strange, I think.

320
00:35:16.018 --> 00:35:29.838
<v Guillaume Hachez>You were talking just now about giving more freedom to schools, and earlier you were talking about the fact that you have chosen to act, or actually is it really a choice to act outside of the school system with your digital skills fund?

321
00:35:30.338 --> 00:35:36.858
<v Guillaume Hachez>Let me speak my mind for a second here. Our political system works in such a layered and modular way that-

322
00:35:36.918 --> 00:35:37.118
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm

323
00:35:37.438 --> 00:35:49.018
<v Guillaume Hachez>... here you are deputy prime minister and you don't really have a say over what goes on in our schools. And so you feel that it's a good thing to have this laboratory outside of the school system to experiment-

324
00:35:49.298 --> 00:35:49.388
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm

325
00:35:49.388 --> 00:35:56.028
<v Guillaume Hachez>... and to then convince schools to include programming. But you don't really have a choice in that matter because you are not ...

326
00:35:56.228 --> 00:35:59.178
<v Guillaume Hachez>Schools are not part of your jurisdiction, so to say.

327
00:35:59.238 --> 00:36:07.338
<v Alexander De Croo>That's true. Now, in the end, we're not the only country where it's organized that way.

328
00:36:07.398 --> 00:36:07.478
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay.

329
00:36:07.698 --> 00:36:13.898
<v Alexander De Croo>It's the same in Germany, and in a lot of countries that are federal countries, education is decentralized.

330
00:36:13.958 --> 00:36:27.618
<v Alexander De Croo>So it would be easier if everything is federal, but then there's also some issues which you would have. So I think what is crucial is that you work together and you have ministers of education who have the same ideas.

331
00:36:28.258 --> 00:36:38.158
<v Alexander De Croo>And that's also part of the effort that I should do. I see a lot of good developments taking place, at least in Flanders, where I have more a view on it.

332
00:36:38.758 --> 00:36:44.064
<v Alexander De Croo>And, as I said before, change in education is slow,

333
00:36:45.154 --> 00:36:57.554
<v Alexander De Croo>because it's a people business, and it's about kids' future, and it's not an environment where it's easy to experiment because no one wants to experiment with their kids.

334
00:36:57.774 --> 00:36:59.953
<v Alexander De Croo>So very often it remains a very conservative world.

335
00:36:59.994 --> 00:37:07.144
<v Guillaume Hachez>So the key to having more girls, more women in computing is to have programming in schools.

336
00:37:07.494 --> 00:37:07.524
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

337
00:37:07.574 --> 00:37:13.074
<v Guillaume Hachez>But schools are very conservative and very slow to change, and they have a lot of inertia.

338
00:37:13.714 --> 00:37:14.254
<v Alexander De Croo>It depends.

339
00:37:14.304 --> 00:37:15.484
<v Guillaume Hachez>So it's not gonna happen very quickly from what you're saying.

340
00:37:15.484 --> 00:37:22.274
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, it depends. It depends. You see some schools where it does happen, and that's the point I want to make about giving schools more autonomy.

341
00:37:22.854 --> 00:37:32.334
<v Alexander De Croo>What you see in the end, it will happen when you have motivated teachers and motivated school directors. And giving them more freedom will help.

342
00:37:32.354 --> 00:37:40.394
<v Alexander De Croo>And you see that you have some schools that are doing very interesting things which are in parallel to the official curriculum that they should follow.

343
00:37:40.894 --> 00:37:51.054
<v Alexander De Croo>My feeling is if you give the schools more autonomy, a lot of things will happen, and it's one of the debates that we have today is,

344
00:37:52.074 --> 00:37:56.214
<v Alexander De Croo>how much autonomy can you give schools, especially in this domain?

345
00:37:56.674 --> 00:37:59.434
<v Guillaume Hachez>We only have about 10 minutes left.

346
00:38:00.254 --> 00:38:06.054
<v Guillaume Hachez>During your first mandate as the minister for the, minister of digital agenda,

347
00:38:06.634 --> 00:38:15.204
<v Guillaume Hachez>you introduced, among other things, a new legislation around open data, a tax shelter for startups, fiscal measures for the circular economy,

348
00:38:15.794 --> 00:38:17.254
<v Guillaume Hachez>and your digital skills plans.

349
00:38:17.814 --> 00:38:27.294
<v Guillaume Hachez>If your party fares well in the next elections, which are the challenges that you would like to take up next as the minister of digital agenda?

350
00:38:27.594 --> 00:38:30.434
<v Alexander De Croo>I'm not sure I will be minister of digital agenda again.

351
00:38:30.504 --> 00:38:30.574
<v Guillaume Hachez>Okay. Right.

352
00:38:30.574 --> 00:38:40.874
<v Alexander De Croo>I would love to, but it's not clear-cut today. I think that one of the main elements is our taxation system in a digital world.

353
00:38:41.474 --> 00:38:53.494
<v Alexander De Croo>Our taxation system has been made in an industrial world, and the way, the technical way we tax and the basis which we tax, I think is becoming more and more obsolete.

354
00:38:53.813 --> 00:39:04.154
<v Alexander De Croo>Today we tax corporate profit, but corporate profit is quite hard to define in a digital world, and it's a very volatile thing in this global economy.

355
00:39:04.894 --> 00:39:16.774
<v Alexander De Croo>And we are not using technology enough to simplify taxes and to make it much more fair. One of the ... You talked about the taxation system for the circular economy.

356
00:39:17.474 --> 00:39:31.334
<v Alexander De Croo>What we use is a very easy system. We said instead of asking you to fill out all types of forms, we just ask the platforms every three months to give us a download of all the transactions that happened, and we'll just tax 5% on it.

357
00:39:31.974 --> 00:39:43.534
<v Alexander De Croo>This is a very efficient way of taxing. There's no way to avoid it. It's a broad base and a small percentage, and it doesn't cost us anything because it's basically being done by the platforms.

358
00:39:44.154 --> 00:39:51.034
<v Alexander De Croo>And so changing our taxation much more in such a way, by using,

359
00:39:51.814 --> 00:40:03.304
<v Alexander De Croo>the technology that you have and doing it much more transaction-based than the way it's being done today, I think is one of the main challenges and could

360
00:40:04.434 --> 00:40:11.894
<v Alexander De Croo>make Belgium an interesting testing ground for all types of innovation, as you see that some countries are doing today.

361
00:40:12.534 --> 00:40:19.234
<v Alexander De Croo>In the end, in the world of today, being big or small doesn't make that much of a difference. It's being fast that makes a difference.

362
00:40:19.574 --> 00:40:22.574
<v Alexander De Croo>If as a country you are fast and you create legislation-

363
00:40:22.734 --> 00:40:26.514
<v Guillaume Hachez>Which goes back to what we were saying about the entrepreneurial mindset for-

364
00:40:26.784 --> 00:40:26.794
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah

365
00:40:26.794 --> 00:40:27.214
<v Guillaume Hachez>... politics.

366
00:40:27.314 --> 00:40:40.514
<v Alexander De Croo>Exactly. Yeah. And that is a role that a country like Belgium can play. We can try to be fast and to be the place where testing out things can happen because we were first in having a regulation.

367
00:40:41.074 --> 00:40:55.734
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right. I have to mention this. A 10 thousand pages long report came out last week saying that Telenet, Proximus, and VOO had a significant place on the market and needed to be more open-

368
00:40:56.094 --> 00:40:56.154
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm

369
00:40:56.293 --> 00:41:04.293
<v Guillaume Hachez>... and, yeah, just overall more open to competition. The decision now lies in the hand of the European Commission.

370
00:41:04.494 --> 00:41:04.694
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm.

371
00:41:05.734 --> 00:41:11.934
<v Guillaume Hachez>Using the internet here in Belgium is much, much more expensive than in neighboring countries like France.

372
00:41:12.514 --> 00:41:20.834
<v Guillaume Hachez>What are you hoping will come out of this, and what are your plans to ... How do we change this situation?

373
00:41:20.894 --> 00:41:26.814
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, maybe a few elements. First of all, the quality of our internet is outstanding.

374
00:41:26.844 --> 00:41:26.984
<v Guillaume Hachez>All right.

375
00:41:26.984 --> 00:41:35.504
<v Alexander De Croo>And so any comparison that you will see, Belgium will be top three in Europe on the quality and on the availability of very high internet speeds.

376
00:41:36.074 --> 00:41:37.624
<v Alexander De Croo>That's the first element. Second element,

377
00:41:38.954 --> 00:41:50.134
<v Alexander De Croo>yes, we are definitely not the cheapest country in Europe. But if you compare it with France and others, you have to look at the total cost of telecom and not just the promotional tariffs that you see very, very often.

378
00:41:50.174 --> 00:42:00.034
<v Alexander De Croo>So it's a different dynamic. Still, I consider that we need more competition in Belgium, and this report makes it quite clear.

379
00:42:00.654 --> 00:42:14.284
<v Alexander De Croo>There's three elements which I think are important in the report. First of all, the third-party access. So this is others using the network to provide commercial services

380
00:42:14.574 --> 00:42:16.864
<v Guillaume Hachez>So for instance, like maybe Orange has access to the-

381
00:42:16.894 --> 00:42:18.714
<v Alexander De Croo>Like Orange is doing today. Yeah, like O-

382
00:42:18.784 --> 00:42:19.854
<v Guillaume Hachez>... fiber, fiber optic network from-

383
00:42:19.974 --> 00:42:23.614
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, and Orange today is using the Telenet and the VOO network to provide-

384
00:42:23.914 --> 00:42:23.924
<v Guillaume Hachez>Sure

385
00:42:23.924 --> 00:42:24.734
<v Alexander De Croo>... their fixed services.

386
00:42:25.034 --> 00:42:28.354
<v Guillaume Hachez>Going back to the fiber network that Proximus had-

387
00:42:28.654 --> 00:42:28.914
<v Alexander De Croo>Mm-hmm

388
00:42:29.134 --> 00:42:30.834
<v Guillaume Hachez>... do you think Orange should have access to that as well?

389
00:42:31.294 --> 00:42:46.634
<v Alexander De Croo>Well, so that's one of the three elements. So first one is on the cable access. The regulator provides a way of having a lower cost of access and which is calculated in a better way, and this is a good thing.

390
00:42:46.694 --> 00:42:58.814
<v Alexander De Croo>It will provide more space for commercial innovation by Orange. Second element is that now it will also force to give third-party access for internet only,

391
00:42:59.534 --> 00:43:11.034
<v Alexander De Croo>and this is a very important element because today, if Mobistar is using the network or Orange is using the network of Telenet, it has to take the triple-play product, whereas today, a lot of people are not interested in triple play.

392
00:43:11.254 --> 00:43:20.453
<v Alexander De Croo>A lot of people say, "Just give me internet, and I will stream anything I want. I do not want digital television, and I definitely don't want fixed landlines."

393
00:43:21.084 --> 00:43:21.114
<v Guillaume Hachez>Landlines.

394
00:43:21.114 --> 00:43:29.914
<v Alexander De Croo>And so this is providing, giving the possibility for other players to provide what they call cord-cutting packages.

395
00:43:29.954 --> 00:43:38.764
<v Alexander De Croo>And the third element is then on fiber, is make it clear that when there's fiber investments, some type of access is required.

396
00:43:39.014 --> 00:43:46.774
<v Alexander De Croo>I think that these are good measures, but we will see how the commission is looking at it. I think they have 30 days to react.

397
00:43:47.414 --> 00:44:03.234
<v Alexander De Croo>But this, together with the auctioning of new spectrum for 4 and 5G, which is going to happen in the next months, could bring a new competitive boost into the Belgium telecom market.

398
00:44:03.794 --> 00:44:09.794
<v Guillaume Hachez>Awesome. All right. We only have, like, one minute left, so let's go for a round of very quick questions.

399
00:44:10.014 --> 00:44:10.434
<v Alexander De Croo>Yeah.

400
